# 1. What is Package Management in Linux, and why is it important?
Package Management is the process of installing, updating, removing, and managing software packages in Linux using a package manager.

It helps users install software easily without manually downloading and configuring files.

> Common Package Managers
APT – Debian/Ubuntu
YUM – CentOS/RHEL 7
DNF – Fedora, RHEL 8+
RPM – Red Hat package format
Zypper – openSUSE
> Why is it Important?
Installs software quickly.
Automatically handles dependencies.
Updates software with security patches.
Removes unwanted packages cleanly.
Saves time and simplifies software management.
> Common Commands (Ubuntu)
sudo apt update          # Update package list
sudo apt install nginx   # Install a package
sudo apt remove nginx    # Remove a package
sudo apt upgrade         # Upgrade installed packages

# 2. What are the different package managers used in Linux distributions (APT, YUM, DNF, RPM)?
A package manager is a tool used to install, update, remove, and manage software packages in Linux. Different Linux distributions use different package managers.

Common Package Managers
...

> APT (Ubuntu/Debian)
// sudo apt update
// sudo apt install nginx
> YUM (RHEL/CentOS 7)
// sudo yum install nginx
> DNF (RHEL 8/9, Fedora)
// sudo dnf install nginx

# 3. How do you install a package in Ubuntu and CentOS/RHEL?
To install a package in Linux, I use the package manager based on the distribution. Ubuntu uses APT, while CentOS/RHEL uses YUM or DNF.

In Ubuntu, I use sudo apt install <package_name>. In CentOS/RHEL, I use sudo yum install <package_name> or sudo dnf install <package_name> depending on the version.


